+# Context for the ADR draft — gathered by adr-keeper for /design-adr, Step 2 (#1051)
+shape: markdown
+number: "068"
+title_hint: "Each Product Feature Has One Feature Issue That Names It and Holds Its Scope of Work, and No Feature in the Model Carries an Issue Number"
+slug: each-feature-has-one-feature-issue-that-names-it-and-no-feature-in-the-model-carries-an-issue-number
+status: proposed
+level: framework
+node_ref: ""
+decided_in: "#1051 (a Chore under #1025, \"/shape-plan reuses an open story for a feature instead of filing a second one\"), from checkpoint 1 of the ontology change for #1050"
+decided_by_note: "Leave the Decided by line empty: it is filled when a person accepts. The brief names the product owner, 2026-11-12, at checkpoint 1 of #1050."
+
+supersedes:
+  - adr: "067"
+    file: 067-a-play-finds-a-features-story-by-the-issue-the-model-names-when-no-plan-file-marks-it.md
+    part: full
+    why: >-
+      ADR 067 put the link in the model: a feature's `issue` names its Story, and a play finds the
+      story by it when the plan file marks none. This decision removes that field and that route.
+      Its parts that live on are restated here in a new form: "never by title" (part 3) and "a
+      closed issue means the feature is built" (part 5, now on the Feature issue, not the Story).
+      Its stop rules (an issue named for two features; a feature in two slices of one plan) go:
+      a second epic now asks the person (part 4).
+
+amends:
+  - adr: "056"
+    file: 056-an-epic-and-a-story-are-marked-by-their-issue-type-with-no-label.md
+    parts:
+      - "Decision §1, \"An Epic is type Epic and a Story is type Story, with no label\": the plan play also files one issue of type Feature for each product feature (a Feature issue), and a Story always sits inside a Feature issue. \"An Epic is never filed as type Feature\" stands. Whether a Feature issue carries a label is not decided here (the brief does not say)."
+      - "Decision §2, \"A play finds an epic or a story by the plan file\": for a feature's work, when the plan file marks none, a play finds the issue of type Feature whose text carries the exact line `Represents: <feature id>`. It still never finds an epic, a Feature issue or a story by its title. An epic is still found only by the plan file."
+    stands: "§3 (the other labels) and the rest of ADR 056."
+    note: "ADR 067's forward pointer on ADR 056 (§2) already exists and stays; the new pointer is added after acceptance."
+  - adr: "048"
+    file: 048-the-product-model-and-the-work-are-two-layers.md
+    parts:
+      - "Decision §1, second bullet: the work layer is Epic, Feature, Story, Spike, Chore and Bug (a Feature issue is new)."
+      - "Decision §1, third bullet, the words \"Domain, Capability, Feature, Slice, Source and ICE are never tracker issues. In the tracker, a Story stands for a Feature\": a product feature now has exactly one Feature issue, which stands for it. Domain, Capability, Slice, Source and ICE are still never tracker issues."
+      - "Decision §3, first bullet, \"It holds stories\": an Epic holds Feature issues, and the Stories sit inside them."
+      - "Decision §3, second bullet, in full (\"A Story represents one feature and delivers it. There is one story for each feature in each epic. When a story is made, it writes its own scope of work from its feature's ICE and its capability's ICE\"): the Feature issue represents the feature, one for each feature in the whole tracker, and holds the scope of work written from those two ICE. A Story is a piece of work inside its Feature issue."
+      - "Consequences, Negative / Risks, the bullet \"One story per feature in each epic means one feature can have several stories across epics\": no longer holds."
+      - "Alternatives Considered, the Feature half of \"Make Capability and Feature tracker issues too\": a Feature is now a tracker issue. Capability stays model only."
+    stands: "§1's other bullets, §2's slice definition, §3's third and fourth bullets (Spike, Chore and Bug under a story; no work item carries an ICE), §4 and §5."
+    note: "ADR 067's forward pointer on ADR 048 (§3, second bullet) already exists and stays."
+  - adr: "049"
+    file: 049-a-slice-is-shaped-then-planned-then-given-its-eval.md
+    parts:
+      - "Decision §1.2, the words \"one story for each feature of the slice\": the plan play finds or files one Feature issue for each feature of the slice that is not yet built, and the Stories sit inside it. A feature is never given a second Feature issue."
+      - "Decision §4, each place where the story holds or gives the scope of work and the Context guidance: the second bullet (\"The story holds the goal, the limits and the expectations in its scope of work, and the Context guidance\"), the third bullet (the context sub-agent \"reads the story's Context guidance\"; \"The builder reads the story\"), the fifth bullet (the eval play \"reads the epic, its stories and the hidden file\") and the last paragraph's bullet (\"When an ICE changes after its story is made, the story and its hidden-file entry are drift\"). Each now reads the Feature issue. The rest of §4 stands: no failure or recovery condition reaches the builder, the read rule, the two times the eval is read, and the handoff plan."
+    stands: "§1.1, §1.3, §2 (one plan for each business intent; a slice that serves two intents has one epic), §3 and §5."
+    note: >-
+      ADR 067's forward pointer on ADR 049 names "§2, the words 'one story for each feature of the
+      slice'", but those words are in §1.2, not §2. The new pointer names §1.2. The old pointer
+      stays (an accepted ADR gains only pointers). ADR 052 and ADR 057 already amend other parts of
+      §4; this amendment touches only the words that name the story as the holder of the scope.
+  - adr: "052"
